ary parts of g of z  and uh the white lines the bright white  lines  are like look like contours based on the  modulus or the abs of the function  here's another one shifted cyclic log  abs  characteristic of this shading function  is around any zero you tend to get a  little color wheel  okay like so here you can see three  simple zeros  and then around a pole you tend to get  concentric rings like that  now if you don't want to do shading of  course you can always add a mesh just  like in a lot of our other functions so  you can either have a mesh that  you put in explicitly or it can be sort  of implied through the shading  now complex array plot will be new in  12.2  all right and so it functions largely  like array plot except it works on  complex data  so here we just have you know some  random complex numbers  and the way they're represented unlike  array plot is not with with grayscale  but with  color and this is the same shading um  coloring scheme and shading scheme  that's used in complex plot  so that's not terribly obvious to you  from this particular graphic  um so here oh sorry so here i'm just  using some options i turn the mesh on so  you can see the  little gray lines between the cells i  change the color function  and none here turns off the shading so  there's no modular base shading here  but just to show you that this is the  same shading scheme as  complex plot i'm going to use complex  array plot to more or less regenerate  the image we saw previously of that  function f  so i'm going to make a table here of  f on a grid i wanted it to be a fine  enough grid that you could  realize that this is the same function  or the same plot as before  but not so fine that it's  indistinguishable from indistinguishable  from the complex plot version  so the coloring scheme is the same  both with the color for the argument and  the shading for the modulus  right now one difference here is when um  when table hits the the singularity or  the pole  we get 